// EXPECT: E0268 — calling a polymorphic procedure, which is not yet instantiable (ADR-0081 §2)
//
// W5 sub-wave 1 delivers the `$T` *surface*: a polymorphic procedure parses, formats, lowers as a
// template and checks clean when declared (see `valid/066`). What it does **not** yet do is instantiate a
// *call* — inferring `$T` from the argument, interning the concrete procedure type, and lowering a
// distinct procedure per instantiation. That is the second sub-wave, and it is real work: instantiation
// multiplies procedures, while the whole compiler currently keys one `ProcId` per source declaration.
//
// So a call is refused, and the refusal is **by design** rather than an unimplemented gap left to
// miscompile — the distinction the refused-body lesson turns on (a test's refused construct must be one
// refused deliberately, not merely absent). E0268 names the boundary: the `$T` machinery is here, the call
// side arrives next.
//
// **This file lowers cleanly**, which is why it belongs in `type-errors/` rather than `imports/invalid/`:
// the E0268 poisons the call expression to `ERROR`, but `main`'s body is otherwise well-formed and lowers
// without a further diagnostic. The polymorphic `id` itself produces no MIR — it is a template, skipped as
// a `#foreign` body is — so nothing here is *unlowerable*, only rejected by sema.
#import "Basic";

/// A perfectly good polymorphic declaration — `valid/066` proves declaring it checks clean. The error is
/// entirely at the call below.
id :: (x: $T) -> T {
    return x;
}

main :: () {
    // The refusal: instantiating this call is the next sub-wave's work.
    y := id(42);
}
